================================================================================
Third-party software
================================================================================

goblin-forge is built on the projects below. None of their source is vendored
into this repository — they are fetched by `cargo` and `bun` at build time —
but a compiled Goblin program *does* statically link some of them, so the
distinction between "used to build" and "shipped inside the output" is called
out for each.

Every licence named here is the one the project itself declares. They were read
from the installed packages rather than recalled, but a licence can change
between versions: verify against the exact versions you ship before relying on
this list.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Linked into compiled output
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These end up inside binaries this compiler produces, which is the case where
attribution obligations follow the artefact rather than the repository.

LLVM is deliberately not in this list. `clang` compiles the IR this compiler
emits, but it is invoked as a *subprocess* — a tool the compiler requires, like
the linker, rather than a library linked into it or into what it produces
(DECISIONS §17). Nothing of LLVM's ends up in a Goblin binary.

  libc                            MIT OR Apache-2.0
  The Rust Project Developers
  https://github.com/rust-lang/libc
  Used by the Goblin runtime, which is statically linked into every program.

  mimalloc                        MIT
  (mimalloc, libmimalloc-sys)
  Microsoft Corporation
  https://github.com/microsoft/mimalloc
  The allocator. Every allocation a compiled program makes goes through it and
  its C object code is linked into every binary; the prelude also publishes its
  entry points under their own C names, so a program can hand the same heap to
  a library. It is the compiler's own allocator too, in the native backend.
  One wrinkle for the paragraph above: mimalloc's C source is vendored inside
  the `libmimalloc-sys` crate and compiled from there. Still fetched by cargo
  rather than kept in this repository, but it is C source in a Rust crate
  rather than a Rust crate, and the licence to read is mimalloc's own.

  cty                             MIT OR Apache-2.0
  https://github.com/japaric/cty
  C type aliases, by way of libmimalloc-sys.

